Caller-owned pages and original synthetic fixtures; no historical book edition,
letter, code-name list, scan or copied implementation is bundled.

Gajewski Solresol monosyllable lexicon
Original work: Boleslas Gajewski, Grammaire du Solresol, 1902; PDF page 4 of
the facsimile identified by the source index as a 1997 republication:
https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b9/Grammaire_du_Solresol.pdf
Source provenance and public-domain record:
https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Fichier:Grammaire_du_Solresol.pdf
PDF SHA-256: c5dda1a678112e54c2de790fe3b7612ba18fef6483ee43d42f8b6f74699d6a34
Cube independently transcribes the complete seven-monosyllable French table,
all seventeen glosses and seven explicit abbreviations. No scan, font, score
or modern English translation is distributed. The finite versioned profile
retains synonyms and source distinctions; it is not a general language translator.

Symbolic alphabet source descriptions
====================================

The Tic-Tac-Toe single-grid and quarter-hour clock profiles independently
implement the published descriptions at https://www.dcode.fr/tic-tac-toe-cipher
and https://www.dcode.fr/clock-cipher, retrieved 2026-11-01. dCode.fr permits reuse
of this page content under its stated Creative Commons Attribution (CC-BY)
terms. Cube's geometry tokens, strict segmentation and validation are independent
adaptations; dCode software, images and services are not included. Full source
identity and effective definition hashes accompany operation results.

Pigpen/Rosicrucian paired-cell correspondence is independently transcribed from
Haverhill Family History Group's Pigpen Code worksheet, page 1. Templars glyph
correspondence is independently transcribed from Bothwell-Gosse/Dickinson,
The Knights Templars, pp.89-90, reproduced by the Grand Lodge of British Columbia
and Yukon. Betamaze correspondence and permitted geometric transformations are
independently transcribed from Terrana Cliff's alphabet, reproduced in Conlangs
DE-Cal, Spring 2006, printed p.289 (PDF p.111). NR Tech Studio's independently
published four-feature Birds on a Wire correspondence is implemented as that
publisher's specific variant, with its B01-B26 code aliases. No source artwork,
fonts, maze puzzles, SVG renderer or third-party implementation is included.

Historical alphabet correspondence data
=======================================

The Alphabetum Kaldeorum source table by Heiko Hahn (original upload 2004-08-21)
was published at https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alphabetum_Kaldeorum.jpg
under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported:
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/ . The independently transcribed
crates/cube-core/src/symbols/alphabet/alphabetum_kaldeorum_hahn_2004_v1.json data
component retains that license. Changes replace drawings with source row/column/
variant IDs, retain all displayed correspondences and U/V alternatives, and select
the first printed form deterministically. Its source and .license file are
available with Cube's source distribution; no chart image or font is included.
Independent software remains under its stated Apache-2.0 license.

Other historical glyph correspondences are independently transcribed from the
cited historical plates or attributed scholarly reconstructions: Trithemius (1518);
Agrippa Book III, chapter 30; Theseus Ambrosius (1539) (Peterson chart); Peterson's
Enochian-3188 chart associated with font version 1.00, 2022-08-27; Moon (1877),
reproduced in N4128R, Figure 1; and Doyle's original 18 illustrated poses in the 1905
Return of Sherlock Holmes, as reproduced in Project Gutenberg 108. Digital source
reproductions are credited to their cited publishers, including Joseph H. Peterson
at Esoteric Archives, Michael Everson's Moon proposal, and Project Gutenberg.
Modern fonts, outlines, source prose, images and story solutions are not included.

